	'case1_m' => 'Terraced house · 1920s · Tomasz Wren',
	'case1_s' => 'Ruth Bell was buying a two-bed 1920s terraced house. The brick lintel over the bay was cracked and had dropped. Tomasz wrote that it needed replacing before winter, and Ruth agreed the repair with the seller before she signed.',
	'case1_t' => 'A cracked lintel, agreed with the seller',
	'case2_m' => 'Corner shop · flat above · Henrik Saari',
	'case2_s' => 'Sana Iqbal was buying a corner shop with a flat above. Water was sitting on the rear felt flat roof. Henrik’s report recommended a roofer’s quote before exchange, and the seller re-covered the roof.',
	'case2_t' => 'Ponding on the rear felt roof, re-covered before exchange',
	'case3_m' => 'Ground-floor flat · 1890s house · Adaeze Nwosu',
	'case3_s' => 'Dev Anand was buying a ground-floor flat in a converted 1890s house. A damp patch sat at the skirting. Adaeze’s readings and a blocked air brick showed condensation, and the fix was small.',
	'case3_t' => 'A damp patch that was condensation, not rising damp',
	'cases_0_b' => 'Ruth Bell asked us to look at a two-bed 1920s terraced house before she signed. Tomasz Wren carried out the house survey. Over the bay, the brick lintel was cracked, and the crack stepped up through the mortar. The lintel had dropped about 4 mm. A viewing lasts about twenty minutes and is spent on light and space, so a drop of that size is easy to miss if nobody is there to photograph the mortar and measure it. The report placed the lintel under structure and movement, with a photograph and a plain sentence. It said the lintel needed replacing before winter, and that Ruth should ask the seller.',
	'cases_0_c' => 'The item sat in the column marked now, with a rough cost range. We do not sell or arrange repairs, and we do not work for the seller or the agent. Ruth agreed the repair with the seller before signing. The report arrived within five working days of the visit, and the 30-minute call walked through that sentence with her. A survey is not a valuation. It told her the lintel had dropped, and that the repair belonged in the conversation before she committed. The rest of the house was written in the same ledger, from the roof to the ground outside. The lintel was the finding that had to be agreed first.',
	'cases_0_h' => 'Ruth Bell, a cracked lintel',
	'cases_1_b' => 'Sana Iqbal was buying a corner shop with a flat above. Henrik Saari surveyed the building. It sits in the commercial work we do: a shop up to 400 m², including the flat, about four hours on site, a full condition report rather than a single-defect letter. Henrik looked at the shopfront, the flat roof, the fire escape and the separation between the shop and the flat. The finding that mattered was ponding on the rear felt flat roof. Water sitting on felt is what the photograph shows, and the sentence under it recommended a roofer\'s quote before exchange.',
	'cases_1_c' => 'We did not gather that quote, and we did not instruct the roofer. We do not sell or arrange repairs. We do not work for the seller or the agent. The seller re-covered the roof. Sana had the report, and the 30-minute call, before exchange. The price of this kind of survey is EUR 1,150, including VAT and travel within 40 km of Elsmere Quay, payable after the report is sent, within 14 days. The case note does not add a storey about what the shop was worth. A survey is not a valuation. It put the ponding in writing while the seller could still re-cover the roof.',
	'cases_1_h' => 'Sana Iqbal, a roof before exchange',
	'cases_2_b' => 'Dev Anand asked about a ground-floor flat in a converted 1890s house. Adaeze Nwosu surveyed it, working as she does on damp and interiors. There was a damp patch at the skirting, the kind of tide mark that gets called rising damp before anyone has taken a reading. Adaeze used a damp meter and looked at the ventilation. A blocked air brick served the same wall. The readings and that blocked air brick showed condensation, not rising damp. The report said so in plain words, with a photograph, and it called the remedy a small fix. The item was not written up as a campaign of opening-up.',
	'cases_2_c' => 'We do not lift fixed floors. Where a carpet corner can be turned, we look behind it, and we say if we could not. We do not sell or arrange repairs. We do not work for the seller or the agent. Dev could take the note into the conversation before he signed: once you commit, repairs are yours, and before that they are something to talk about. A survey is not a valuation, and a damp meter reading is not a test of the drains. The letter of the finding was short and specific. Condensation, a blocked air brick, a small fix.',
	'cases_2_h' => 'Dev Anand, condensation at the skirting',
