	'why_h' => 'Why survey before you commit',
	'why_l' => 'Why',
	'why_p1' => 'A viewing lasts about twenty minutes, and most of that time goes on light, room sizes and how the place feels to stand in. It does not go on the brick lintel over the bay, the stain behind the wardrobe, the way a floor falls toward the front wall, or the ponding you cannot see from the street. Those are the parts that decide whether a repair is small or whether it should be talked through before anyone signs.',
	'why_p2' => 'A survey is a trained eye on the fabric for 90 minutes to half a day, from the roof edge and the gutters down to the floor joists, the air bricks and the drains we can see. We measure crack widths, take damp readings, photograph each finding and write one plain sentence beside it. The report is not a valuation, and it is not a promise that every hidden defect will appear. It is a record of what could be seen on that visit, set out so you can read it without a specialist beside you.',
	'why_p3' => 'The reason to do this before you sign is practical. Once you commit, the repairs are yours to pay for and to organise. Before you commit, the same findings are something to put in front of the seller: a lintel to replace before winter, a flat roof to re-cover, a blocked air brick rather than a damp-proof course. We write the condition down, then leave the decision with you.',
	'wordmark' => 'Noki Surveyors',
);
